\cataloguefield{subsection}{Capability Overview}

Health Information Management maintains health information as meaningful governed content rather than isolated persisted values. Its children distinguish the content itself from the structures that explain it, lifecycle and history, discovery aids, contextual groupings, relationships and retrieval. These distinctions let an architect ask whether information exists, what it means, which state applies and whether it may be obtained without conflating those questions.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Definition / governed semantic detail}

Manage health information as governed, semantically meaningful content.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Intent}

Preserve usable health information whose meaning, relationships and temporal standing can be understood.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Boundary}

Discoverability is not permission, compartment membership is not an access decision, and correlation is not identity or clinical interpretation. Current state and immutable historical operational truth remain distinct.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Preserved v0.1 Review Matters}

Canonical BC evidence/\allowbreak{}context/\allowbreak{}consumption semantics are preserved in Markdown; the current governed RADS relationship types do not establish their machine-readable encoding. No stronger runtime or structural relation is substituted.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Scope / Contained Elements}
\begingroup\small\setlength{\TableWidth}{\dimexpr\linewidth-6\tabcolsep-4\arrayrulewidth\relax}
\begin{longtable}{|>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.52\TableWidth}|>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.2\TableWidth}|>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.28\TableWidth}|}
\hline\rowcolor{TableHead}\textbf{Element / page} &amp; \textbf{Type / tier} &amp; \textbf{Element / placement status}\\\hline\endfirsthead
