\cataloguefield{subsection}{Consent responsibility}
\nameref{cap:bec-client-privacy} retains client consent directives and FEAT-EM-09. This branch respects that responsibility without duplicating it.
\cataloguefield{subsection}{Capability Overview}

Govern patient health-information restrictions, filtering and disclosure using established information-access authority, client privacy/\allowbreak{}consent determinations and applicable security controls.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Definition — Reviewed candidate}

Govern patient health-information restrictions, filtering and disclosure using established information-access authority, client privacy/\allowbreak{}consent determinations and applicable security controls.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Boundary — retained v0.1 source explanation}

Security context is operational context and does not automatically become resource content. Security-context validation has no invented one-to-one mapping to propagation, and audit recording is not an assurance conclusion.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Harmonia Architectural Relevance — retained v0.1 source explanation}

Assigned policy enforcement and attributable context are substantive Core responsibilities across managed operations.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Boundary}

Information administration does not confer authority over the represented person, organisation, service, place, device or clinical activity. Client consent directives remain owned by Client Privacy Guardrail Management (FEAT-EM-09); this capability references and respects those determinations without defining a second consent owner.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Proposed Placement Rationale}

Responsibility-preserving placement under the human-reviewed BEC hierarchy; the structural assertion remains Proposed.


