HUMAN ADJUDICATION REQUIRED: Canonical assurance against a governed subject is not independent review of the assurance service itself.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Function Overview}

Evidence assessment applies the established evaluative basis to the available trustworthy material. It produces assessed detail rather than treating source availability or successful processing as a conclusion. Explicit gaps and qualifications preserve what the evidence cannot establish for subsequent adjudication.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Definition / governed semantic detail}

\begin{itemize}

\item
\textbf{Canonical Name}: \texttt{\seqsplit{Assess Assurance Evidence}}.



\item
\textbf{Definition}: Evaluates the available trustworthy evidence against the applicable assurance criteria and establishes what that evidence demonstrates for each relevant criterion or assessment concern.



\item
\textbf{Boundary}: Assessment remains distinct from adjudication. It may identify satisfaction, exception, insufficiency or other criterion-level results where applicable; no universal result taxonomy is established. Quantitative, qualitative, compound, temporal and confidence-bearing assessment remain possible. \textbf{Insufficient evidence is not equivalent to either satisfaction or non-satisfaction.}



\end{itemize}




\cataloguefield{subsection}{Preserved v0.1 Review Matters}

No contextual-view placement is established.


